        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          If you walk on a treadmill wearing a fitness tracker, a smartwatch or with a phone in your
          pocket, will the steps count? In most cases, yes. Wrist trackers do not need you to move
          across the ground; they count steps from the rhythmic swing of your arm, which happens on
          a belt exactly as it does on a pavement. The exceptions are predictable: holding the
          handrails, resting your hands on a desk over a walking pad, or carrying something in the
          hand that wears the tracker. Distance is the harder part. Indoors there is no useful GPS,
          so every device has to estimate distance from your steps and stride, and that is where
          tracker, phone and treadmill console start to disagree. This page explains how the
          counting works, what breaks it, how to calibrate it, and which figure deserves your
          trust.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Does a Fitness Tracker Count Steps on a Treadmill? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Yes — wrist trackers and smartwatches usually count steps on a treadmill,
            because they detect steps from your arm swing, not from moving across the ground.</strong>
            The count drops sharply if you hold the handrails or keep your hands still, and the
            distance a tracker shows indoors is an estimate from your stride, not a GPS
            measurement.
          </p>
          <p>
            Phones count treadmill steps too, as long as they are on your body — in a pocket, a
            waistband or an armband. A phone sitting in the console's holder counts nothing, because
            it is not moving. And a phone or watch trying to use GPS on a treadmill will record
            either no distance or a meaningless scatter, because you are not going anywhere.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical rules are short. Let your arms swing naturally where you can safely do so.
            Start an indoor walk or indoor run workout rather than an outdoor one, so the device
            estimates distance from motion instead of looking for GPS. Expect the tracker's distance
            and the console's distance to differ by a few percent, and sometimes more. For steps,
            trust a tracker on a freely swinging arm; for distance, trust a console you have checked;
            for calories, trust neither too much.
          </p>
          <p>
            The rest of this page covers each of those points, including how Strava and similar apps
            record a treadmill session.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-counts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. How Does a Wrist Tracker Count Steps on a Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            A wrist tracker counts treadmill steps the same way it counts any steps: an accelerometer
            senses the repeating pattern of your arm swinging and the small jolt of each foot strike
            travelling up your body, and software turns that rhythm into a step count.
          </p>
          <p>
            An accelerometer is a tiny motion sensor that measures acceleration in three directions.
            When you walk, your wrist moves in a regular pendulum pattern — forward and back once for
            every two steps — and each foot strike adds a small shock. The tracker's software looks
            for that rhythm, filters out motion that does not fit it (typing, stirring a pan, waving)
            and counts what is left. Many devices also need a short run of consistent steps before
            they start counting, so a couple of isolated steps may not register.
          </p>
          <p>
            None of this depends on your body moving forward through space. On a treadmill, you stay
            in one place while the belt moves, but your arms and legs go through the same motion as
            walking down a street. That is why the step count on a treadmill is usually similar to
            what the same tracker would record outdoors for the same number of real steps.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two details are worth knowing. First, the arm swing on a treadmill is sometimes smaller
            than outdoors, particularly at slow speeds or when people are watching a screen, and a
            very small swing can be harder for the software to recognise. Second, devices differ in
            their algorithms, so two trackers worn on the same walk can give slightly different
            counts. Neither is necessarily wrong; both are estimating.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you want to know how many steps a treadmill mile should take for someone of your
            height and stride — useful for sanity-checking a tracker — our guide to
            <a href="/how-many-steps-in-a-mile-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how many steps are in a mile on a treadmill</a>
            explains the arithmetic and how to measure your own figure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="handrails" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Why Does Holding the Handrails Stop the Step Count?</h2>
<p>
            Holding the handrails stops a wrist tracker counting steps because it stops your wrist
            swinging. With your hand clamped to a rail, the tracker sees almost none of the motion it
            uses to recognise walking, so it may miss a large share of your steps or record almost
            none at all.
          </p>
          <p>
            Your legs are still taking every step, of course, and some of the jolt of each foot
            strike still reaches the wrist. Some devices pick up enough of that to keep counting
            partially; others do not. The result is unpredictable, which is the real problem: you
            cannot apply a correction factor to a count that might be 90% right one session and 30%
            right the next.
          </p>
          <p>
            The same thing happens with other still-hand situations:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Resting a hand on the console</strong> or a tablet while you walk.</li>
            <li><strong>Typing on a desk</strong> over a walking pad or under-desk treadmill.</li>
            <li><strong>Holding a phone, book or drink</strong> in the hand that wears the
            tracker.</li>
            <li><strong>Pushing a pram or a trolley</strong> outdoors, for the same reason.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            There are good reasons to hold the rails sometimes — getting on and off, speed changes,
            and throughout if you need support for balance — and none of them should be given up to
            protect a step count. If you do hold on routinely, a phone in a pocket or a clip-on
            tracker at the waist counts body movement rather than arm swing and copes much better.
            If you can walk safely hands-free, our guide to
            <a href="/should-you-hold-the-handrails-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether to hold the handrails on a treadmill</a>
            explains why letting go also gives you a better workout, and how to do it safely.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="phone" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Does Your Phone Count Steps on a Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            Yes — a phone counts steps on a treadmill if it is on your body, such as in a trouser
            pocket, a waistband belt or an armband, because its motion sensor feels each step. A
            phone resting in the console's holder or on a shelf counts nothing.
          </p>
          <p>
            Most modern smartphones have a built-in accelerometer and a low-power motion processor
            that counts steps in the background, which is where the step figure in your phone's
            health app comes from. Carried close to your hips, a phone feels the up-and-down motion
            of your body with each step, which is a strong, regular signal. That is why a phone in a
            pocket is often a decent step counter on a treadmill even when you hold the rails, since
            it does not depend on arm swing.
          </p>
          <p>
            Where it goes wrong:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>On the console.</strong> Most people put their phone in the treadmill's
            media holder to watch something, and a phone there sees only the vibration of the
            machine. Some phones may count a few phantom steps from strong vibration; most count
            very little. Either way, it is not your walk.</li>
            <li><strong>In a loose bag or a jacket pocket.</strong> Motion that is damped or
            irregular is harder to count.</li>
            <li><strong>In your hand.</strong> Holding a phone while walking stops the natural arm
            swing on that side, although the phone itself may still count reasonably well.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>GPS distance is useless indoors.</strong> If you start a workout on your phone
            that relies on GPS, it will record a distance close to zero, or a small scribble caused by
            GPS drift inside the building, because your location is not changing. For treadmill
            distance, either use a mode that estimates from steps, or take the figure from the
            console.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="indoor-mode" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Do You Need to Start an Indoor Walk or Run Workout?</h2>
<p>
            You do not need to start a workout for most trackers to count your treadmill steps,
            because all-day step counting usually runs in the background — but you should start an
            indoor walk or indoor run workout if you want a sensible distance, pace and heart-rate
            record for the session.
          </p>
          <p>
            Most watches and many fitness bands have an indoor walk, indoor run or treadmill workout
            type. The general idea is the same across brands: the device stops looking for GPS,
            estimates distance from your steps and its model of your stride, and records the
            session as a workout with heart rate, duration and an estimated pace. Names and options
            vary, so check your device's workout list.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Why not just use an outdoor workout?</strong> An outdoor walk or run started
            indoors will look for GPS. Depending on the device, you get a distance near zero, a
            wandering trace from GPS drift, or a fallback to motion-based distance with a confused
            record. The indoor mode avoids all of that.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Walk or run?</strong> Pick the mode that matches what you are doing. Devices
            commonly use different assumptions about stride for walking and running, so recording a
            walk as a run, or the reverse, tends to make the distance estimate worse. For mixed
            run-walk sessions, choose the mode for the activity you spend most time in.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Connected treadmills.</strong> Some watches can pair directly with some
            treadmills and take the machine's speed and distance instead of estimating. Where that
            works, it removes most of the disagreement covered below. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-connect-apple-watch-to-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">connecting an Apple Watch to a treadmill</a>
            covers one example in detail; other brands have their own approaches, and support
            depends on both the watch and the machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="distance-disagree" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Why Does My Tracker Distance Not Match the Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            Your tracker's distance does not match the treadmill because both are estimates made in
            different ways: the tracker multiplies your step count by a stride length it has learned
            or assumed, and the console multiplies belt speed by time, using a speed it may infer
            rather than measure.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The tracker's side.</strong> Indoors, distance is steps times stride. If the step
            count is off — handrails, still hands, a missed start — the distance is off by the same
            proportion. If the stride length is wrong, every step carries the error. Many watches
            learn your stride from outdoor GPS walks and runs, and your treadmill stride may not
            match: people often take slightly shorter, quicker steps on a belt, and incline
            shortens the stride further. A device that has never seen an outdoor GPS session may be
            working from a stride guessed from your height.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The treadmill's side.</strong> The console calculates distance from belt speed,
            and many home machines infer that speed from a sensor on the roller or from the motor
            setting. A belt that slips under load, a roller sensor slightly out, or a calibration that
            was never quite right can make the console a few percent long or short. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-calibration/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill calibration</a>
            explains how to check it with a tape measure and a stopwatch.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Incline.</strong> On an incline, the console still reports belt distance, which
            is correct, while your shorter uphill stride means more steps per mile. A tracker using a
            fixed stride then over-reads distance on steep walks. This is one of the biggest sources
            of disagreement for people who walk at high grades.
          </p>
          <p>
            A gap of a few percent between the two is normal and does not mean either device is
            faulty. Larger gaps usually trace back to one of the causes above, most often handrails
            or an unchecked console.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stride-calibration" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. How Do You Calibrate a Tracker for Treadmill Walking?</h2>
<p>
            You calibrate a tracker for treadmill walking by giving it a better stride length —
            either by correcting the distance at the end of an indoor workout where your device
            offers that, by entering a measured stride length where your app allows it, or by doing
            some outdoor GPS walks so it can learn your stride.
          </p>
          <p>
            Calibration only helps if the reference is right, so check the treadmill first. A tracker
            calibrated to a console that reads 5% long will faithfully reproduce that 5% error
            everywhere. Once you are confident in the belt, these are the common approaches:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Post-workout correction.</strong> Some watches offer to let you enter the
            treadmill's distance at the end of an indoor run or walk, and use the correction to
            improve future indoor estimates. Where the option exists, it is the easiest
            method.</li>
            <li><strong>Manual stride length.</strong> Some apps let you enter walking and running
            stride lengths in their settings. Measure yours: walk a known distance — the treadmill
            for a measured 0.25 miles, say — count the steps, and divide. A quarter-mile is 1,320
            feet, so 600 steps would be a stride of 2.2 feet, or about 26 inches.</li>
            <li><strong>Outdoor GPS sessions.</strong> Many watches refine their stride model from
            outdoor GPS workouts. A few outdoor walks or runs at a similar pace to your treadmill
            sessions can improve indoor accuracy over time.</li>
            <li><strong>Foot pods and shoe sensors.</strong> Some running watches can pair with a
            small sensor on the shoe, which measures foot movement directly and tends to give better
            indoor distance once calibrated. It is an extra purchase and only worth it if you care
            about the number.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Recalibrate when something changes: a new pair of shoes, a shift from walking to running,
            or a habit of steep incline sessions. Your stride is not a fixed property; it changes with
            speed, grade and fatigue.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="strava" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Does Strava Track Treadmill Walks and Runs?</h2>
<p>
            Yes, Strava can record treadmill walks and runs, but it cannot use GPS for them, so the
            distance comes from a watch's motion-based estimate, a connected treadmill or app, or a
            figure you enter yourself.
          </p>
          <p>
            The common routes are recording an indoor workout on a watch that syncs to Strava,
            recording with the Strava app on a device that can estimate indoor distance, or linking
            a treadmill platform or training app that pushes completed sessions across. In every
            case, the distance Strava shows is only as good as its source — so the handrail,
            stride and calibration points above apply in full. If the source counted too few steps,
            the Strava activity is short by the same amount.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two habits keep a Strava record tidy. Mark the activity as a treadmill or indoor
            activity, so it is not treated like an outdoor GPS run; and if the distance is clearly
            wrong, check the activity's edit options, where Strava has offered a way to correct
            treadmill distance. Pace follows whichever distance is saved. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-connect-apple-watch-to-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">connecting an Apple Watch to a treadmill</a>
            walks through the Strava routes for that watch in more detail.
          </p>
          <p>
            The same principles apply to other fitness platforms: without GPS, a treadmill session
            is a motion estimate or a number you supply, and the platform can only display what it
            was given.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="walking-pads" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. What About Walking Pads and Desk Walking?</h2>
<p>
            Walking pads and under-desk treadmills are the worst case for wrist trackers, because
            your hands are usually on a keyboard or mouse, so the tracker sees little arm swing and
            may badly under-count. A phone in a pocket or a clip-on tracker at the waist usually
            does far better.
          </p>
          <p>
            Desk walking also tends to be slow, often well under 2 mph, with short, shuffling steps.
            That is a gentle signal for any motion sensor, and some trackers have more difficulty
            recognising very slow walking than a brisk pace. Owners commonly report that a session
            they know was a couple of miles shows up as a fraction of that on the wrist.
          </p>
          <p>
            A few approaches help:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Move the counter to your body.</strong> A phone in a trouser pocket or a
            tracker clipped to a waistband counts hip movement rather than arm swing.</li>
            <li><strong>Use the pad's own figures.</strong> Many walking pads show distance and
            sometimes an estimated step count on a display or in an app. That step figure is
            usually derived from distance and an assumed stride, so it is an estimate, but it is
            unaffected by your hands.</li>
            <li><strong>Take short hands-free breaks.</strong> A few minutes of walking with arms
            swinging, while reading or on a call, is counted normally.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you are deciding between a walking pad and a full treadmill, our comparison of
            <a href="/walking-pad-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pads and treadmills</a>
            covers speed ranges, handrails and what each is good for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-trust" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Which Number Should You Trust: Tracker, Phone or Console?</h2>
<p>
            For steps, trust a wrist tracker on a freely swinging arm or a phone in your pocket; for
            distance, trust the treadmill console once you have checked it; and for calories, treat
            every device's figure as a rough estimate.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Measure</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Usually most reliable</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Main thing that breaks it</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Steps</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Wrist tracker with free arm swing, or phone in pocket</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Holding rails, hands on a desk, phone on the console</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Distance</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treadmill console, once checked</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt slip, poor calibration</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Pace and speed</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treadmill console</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Same as distance</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Heart rate</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Chest strap, then a well-fitted watch</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Loose fit, grip sensors at running speed</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Calories</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">None precisely</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Wrong body weight, holding rails, model assumptions</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>Steps belong to the tracker</strong> because the treadmill does not count them.
            Any step figure on a treadmill console is usually distance divided by an assumed stride,
            which is a guess about you. A tracker on a moving wrist is at least counting your actual
            movement.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Distance belongs to the console</strong> because the belt really did travel that
            far, give or take the machine's calibration. A tracker indoors is estimating from stride,
            which is one step further removed. If the two disagree by a lot, check the belt with the
            method in our calibration guide before deciding which to believe; our guide to whether
            <a href="/is-my-treadmill-speed-accurate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">your treadmill speed is accurate</a>
            covers the symptoms of a console that is off.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Consistency matters more than precision.</strong> For most people, the useful
            question is whether this week was more active than last week, and any device answers that
            well as long as you use it the same way each time. Pick one source for each measure and
            stick with it, rather than switching to whichever looks best on the day.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Fitbit count steps on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Wrist-worn trackers, Fitbit devices included, generally count treadmill steps from the swing of your arm, so they work on a treadmill much as they do outdoors. The count drops if you hold the handrails or keep your hands still. Distance indoors is estimated from your stride, so it may differ from the console. Check your device's documentation for its indoor options.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Apple Watch count steps on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, a smartwatch on your wrist counts treadmill steps from arm motion, as it does outdoors, and the count falls if you grip the rails. For a proper workout record, start an indoor walk or indoor run rather than an outdoor one, so the watch estimates distance from motion instead of looking for GPS. Some treadmills can also share their distance with the watch directly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does my phone count steps on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if it is on your body. A phone in a trouser pocket, waistband or armband feels each step and counts it, even if you hold the rails. A phone in the console holder counts almost nothing because it is not moving with you. Any GPS distance on a treadmill is useless, because your location does not change.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my tracker not count steps when I hold the treadmill handrails?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Wrist trackers recognise walking from the rhythm of your arm swing. With your hand on a rail, your wrist barely moves, so the tracker sees little of the pattern it looks for and may miss many steps or almost all of them. A phone in a pocket or a waist clip counts body movement instead and copes far better.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Strava track treadmill runs?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, Strava can record treadmill runs and walks, but without GPS the distance comes from a watch or phone estimate, a connected treadmill or app, or a number you enter. Mark the activity as a treadmill or indoor activity, and correct the distance in the activity's edit options if it is clearly wrong. Pace follows the saved distance.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I trust my watch or the treadmill for distance?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually the treadmill, once you have checked it, because the belt really travelled that distance, while a watch indoors estimates from your step count and stride. A gap of a few percent is normal. If the gap is large, check the belt speed with a tape measure and stopwatch, then calibrate the watch to the treadmill.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes With Trackers on a Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Almost every bad treadmill step count or distance traces back to one of these four habits.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Holding the rails and trusting the count</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A wrist tracker counts steps from arm swing, so a hand clamped to the rail can make it miss a large share of steps unpredictably. If you need the rails, keep using them and move the step counting to a phone in your pocket or a waist clip instead. If you do not need them, let go and swing your arms.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Starting an outdoor GPS workout indoors</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">An outdoor walk or run looks for GPS, and on a treadmill that gives a distance near zero or a wandering trace from signal drift. Start the indoor walk, indoor run or treadmill workout type instead, so the device estimates distance from motion and records the session sensibly.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Calibrating the watch to an unchecked console</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Telling your watch that the treadmill is right only helps if the treadmill is right. A console that reads a few percent long will pass that error to every future indoor estimate. Check the belt speed with the tape-and-stopwatch method first, then calibrate the watch to it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the phone in the console holder</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A phone in the media holder sees the vibration of the machine, not your steps, and the step count for the session ends up close to nothing. If the phone is your step counter, carry it in a pocket or armband and use a tablet or the console for entertainment instead.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

